'''Unakorowake Shrine''' (宇奈己呂和気神社) is a {{ill|Shinto shrine|ja|神社|12=draft}} located in {{ill|Kōriyama|ja|郡山市|12=draft}}, {{ill|Fukushima Prefecture|ja|福島県|12=draft}}. It is a {{ill|Shikinai shrine|ja|式内社|12=draft}} ({{ill|Myōjin taisha|ja|名神大社|fr|Myōjin Taisha|zh|名神大社|12=draft}}), and its former shrine ranking under the {{ill|Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|ja|近代社格制度|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|12=draft}} was {{ill|Gōsha|ja|郷社|12=draft}}.
[[Unakoro Waki Shrine]] is a regional [[Sōja (Shinto)|Sōja shrine]]. It enshrines many kami of the local region.<!-- Content taken from [[Sōja (Shinto)]] explaining the concept of it -->

It contrasts with provincial Soja shrines which enshrine all the kami of a province<ref>https://archive.ph/wip/XtZfZ</ref>


== Enshrined Deities ==
'''Main deity'''
* '''{{ill|Seoritsuhime|ja|瀬織津比売命|12=draft}}''' - One of the {{ill|Haraedo-no-Ōkami|ja|祓戸大神|12=draft}}

'''Secondary deity'''
* {{ill|Hachiman|ja|八幡神|12=draft}}: Hondawake-no-Mikoto ({{ill|Emperor Ōjin|ja|応神天皇|12=draft}})

== History ==
=== Founding ===
According to ancient records, during the reign of {{ill|Emperor Kōnin|ja|光仁天皇|12=draft}}, disturbances caused by the {{ill|Emishi|ja|蝦夷|12=draft}} were widespread in the Mutsu Province. In the first year of {{ill|Ten'ō|ja|天応|12=draft}} ({{ill|781|ja|781st year|12=draft}}), the court dispatched {{ill|Fujiwara no Oguro|ja|藤原小黒麿|12=draft}} as inspector of Mutsu and Dewa, but he failed to suppress the unrest. The following year, in the first year of {{ill|Enryaku|ja|延暦|12=draft}} ({{ill|782|ja|782nd year|12=draft}}), the newly enthroned {{ill|Emperor Kanmu|ja|桓武天皇|12=draft}} sent {{ill|Ōtomo no Yakamochi|ja|大伴家持|12=draft}} to the region as an inspector and commander. After seeking divine guidance on {{ill|Takahatayama|ja|高旗山|12=draft}}, Yakamochi established a shrine to enshrine Seoritsuhime, whose divine power reportedly aided in subduing the unrest.

Later, in the 3rd year of Enryaku ({{ill|784|ja|784th year|12=draft}}), the shrine was moved from Takahatayama to its current location in Yamazaki. At that time, {{ill|Hachiman|ja|八幡大神|12=draft}} (Hondawake-no-Mikoto) was enshrined as a secondary deity. A stone shrine (okumiya) remains on the summit of Takahatayama, and an information board at the mountain trailhead near {{ill|Genda Onsen|ja|源田温泉|12=draft}} recounts this history.

According to shrine records, the shrine’s origins trace back to the 11th year of Emperor Kinmei ({{ill|550|ja|550th year|12=draft}}?). During that time, Seoritsuhime was believed to have manifested on Takahatayama's summit. Seoritsuhime, identified as a deity born from the purification of {{ill|Izanagi|ja|伊耶那岐命|12=draft}} at Awagihara, was venerated for her ability to cleanse impurities and pacify disturbances. 

=== Historical Overview ===
As recorded in the {{ill|Shoku Nihongi|ja|続日本後紀|12=draft}}, the shrine was granted the rank of Junior Fifth Rank, Lower Grade, in the 14th year of {{ill|Jōwa (Heian period)|ja|承和|12=draft}} ({{ill|847|ja|847th year|12=draft}}). According to the {{ill|Ruijū Kokushi|ja|類聚国史|12=draft}}, the shrine's rank was elevated to Senior Fifth Rank, Lower Grade, in the 11th year of {{ill|Jōgan|ja|貞観|12=draft}} ({{ill|869|ja|869th year|12=draft}}).

In {{ill|927|ja|927th year|12=draft}}, the shrine was listed as a {{ill|Myōjin taisha|ja|名神大社|fr|Myōjin Taisha|zh|名神大社|12=draft}} in the {{ill|Engishiki|ja|延喜式|12=draft}} and became the main shrine of the three {{ill|Shikinai shrines|ja|式内社|12=draft}} in the Asaka District, receiving deep veneration from both rulers and local residents. It was also designated the total shrine (sōsha) for the {{ill|33 communities of Asaka|ja|安積三十三郷|12=draft}}.

During the {{ill|Kamakura period|ja|鎌倉時代|12=draft}}, {{ill|Kudō Suketsune|ja|工藤祐経|12=draft}}, appointed as local governor, introduced deities from the {{ill|Mishima Taisha|ja|三嶋大社|12=draft}} and other shrines in Izu Province as subordinate shrines. The shrine was further restored by local rulers such as {{ill|Ashina clan|ja|蘆名氏|12=draft}} and {{ill|Gamo Ujisato|ja|蒲生氏郷|12=draft}}, and it received land grants and other support.

=== typeings ===
* {{ill|Jōwa (Heian period)|ja|承和|12=draft}} 14 ({{ill|847|ja|847th year|12=draft}}): Junior Fifth Rank, Lower Grade ({{ill|Shoku Nihongi|ja|続日本後紀|12=draft}})
* {{ill|Jōgan|ja|貞観|12=draft}} 11 ({{ill|869|ja|869th year|12=draft}}): Senior Fifth Rank, Lower Grade ({{ill|Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku|ja|日本三代実録|12=draft}})

== Shrine Grounds ==
=== Main Shrine ===
The shrine comprises a haiden, heiden, and a covered three-bay honden in {{ill|Miya-zukuri|ja|流造|12=draft}} style. The honden features three doors, symbolizing the deities enshrined. Presently, the shrine honors two deities, with one seat left vacant.

<gallery widths="200px" heights="150px">
File:Honden of Unakorowake-jinja 1.JPG|Honden (Three-bay miya-zukuri style)
File:Unakorowake-jinja 1.JPG|Haiden
</gallery>

=== Torii and Approach ===
A stream flows beneath the torii. The approach features memorial stones, subsidiary shrines, and a shrine office.

<gallery>
File:Suitengu shrine of Unakorowake-jinja.JPG|Water deity shrine
File:Main approach to Unakorowake-jinja 2.JPG|Main approach
File:Torii gate of Unakorowake-jinja.JPG|Torii
</gallery>

=== Subsidiary Shrines ===
The shrine precinct includes various stone shrines dedicated to deities such as {{ill|Mishima Taisha|ja|三嶋神社|12=draft}}, {{ill|Izu-san Shrine|ja|伊豆山神社|12=draft}}, {{ill|Hakone Shrine|ja|箱根神社|12=draft}}, and others.

== Festivals ==
The Spring Festival is held annually from {{ill|April 2|ja|4月2日|12=draft}}, and the Autumn Festival occurs from {{ill|September 18|ja|9月18日|12=draft}}. Historical records indicate that events such as horseback archery were once part of the celebrations.

== Access ==
'''Address'''
* {{ill|Fukushima Prefecture|ja|福島県|12=draft}}, {{ill|Kōriyama|ja|郡山市|12=draft}}, Mihota-machi Hachiman, Kamino-dai 76

=== Public Transportation ===
* From {{ill|Koriyama Station|ja|郡山駅|12=draft}}, take the No. 2 bus bound for Nagahama (approx. 35 minutes). Alight at Mihota Community Center and walk immediately.

=== By Car ===
* About 10 minutes from {{ill|Koriyama-Minami IC|ja|郡山南インターチェンジ|12=draft}}.

== References ==
* ''Asaka no Jinjashi'' (Fukushima Prefecture Shrine Bureau, Kōriyama Branch, 2001), pp. 181–183.
* ''Zenkoku Jinja Meikan, Vol. 1'' (National Shrine Directory, 1977).

