File:Inside of Haiden of Unakorowake-jinja 1.JPG|Interior of the haiden<br>The shrine mirror reveals the three doors of the honden.
File:Inside of Haiden of Unakorowake-jinja 2.JPG|Plaques reading "Asaka Sōsha, Unakorowake Shrine" and "Hachiman Shrine."
File:Inside of Haiden of Unakorowake-jinja 4.JPG|Plaque commemorating Itō Hirobumi’s donation: "Heihaku Fee: Three Yen, Councillor Itō Hirobumi."
File:Ema board of "The story of Kiyomasa Kato hunting a tiger".JPG|Large ema: "Kato Kiyomasa hunting a tiger."
File:Ema board of "The story of Susanowo slaying Yamata-no-Orochi".JPG|Large ema: "Susanoo slaying Yamata-no-Orochi."
</gallery>

'''Other structures'''
<gallery>
File:Kaguraden of Unakorowake-jinja.JPG|'''Kaguraden''' (sacred dance stage)
</gallery>

=== Torii and approach ===
Passing through the '''torii''', visitors cross a small stream. To the west of the approach are a memorial stone and the '''Suitengū''' (dedicated to [[:en:Ame-no-Minakanushi|Ame-no-Minakanushi]]), while the shrine office lies to the right.  
<gallery>
File:Suitengu shrine of Unakorowake-jinja.JPG|Suitengū shrine within the grounds.
File:Memorial stone of Unakorowake-jinja.JPG|Memorial stone along the approach.
File:Main approach to Unakorowake-jinja 2.JPG|The shrine approach.
File:Main approach to Unakorowake-jinja 1.JPG|The stone bridge and approach.
File:Torii gate of Unakorowake-jinja.JPG|The torii gate.
</gallery>

== Auxiliary and subordinate shrines ==
To the west of the main shrine building lies the '''Yōsan Shrine''' (dedicated to Uka-no-Mitama-no-Kami and Wakumusubi-no-Kami) and a memorial shrine for the war dead. Additional stone shrines enshrine deities such as Mishima Shrine, Izusan Shrine, Hakone Shrine (all brought by Kudō Suketsune), Aosa Shrine, Yama Shrine, Inari Shrine, Kasuga Shrine, Tenman-gū, Itsukushima Shrine, and others.

== Festivals ==
The spring grand festival takes place from April 2 to April 4, and the autumn grand festival occurs from September 18 to September 20. In the past, events such as yabusame (horseback archery) and mikoshi processions were held but were discontinued following the {{ill|Kakitsu Rebellion|ja|嘉吉の乱|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kakitsu Rebellion|qq=|1=Kakitsu Incident|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q5365139}} in 1441 and the [[:en:Kanshō famine|Great Famine of Chōroku and Kanshō]] in 1459. While attempts were made to revive the mikoshi processions under the protection of the Tanba clan during the Edo period, these rituals are no longer performed.

== Access ==
'''location'''
* 76 Kamino-dai, Hachiman, Mihota-machi, Kōriyama City, Fukushima Prefecture, Japan.

=== By bus ===
* From [[:en:Kōriyama Station (Fukushima)|Koriyama Station (Fukushima)]] (JR East Tōhoku Shinkansen), take the No. 2 bus bound for Naganuma (approx. 35 minutes) and alight at "Mihota Public Hall." The shrine is a short walk away. Check timetables for details.

=== By car ===
* About 10 minutes from {{ill|Kōriyama Minami Interchange (Fukushima)|ja|郡山南インターチェンジ (福島県)|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kōriyama Minami Interchange (Fukushima)|qq=|1=Koriyama-minami interchange|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q11643156}}.

