'''Unakorowake Shrine''' (宇奈己呂和気神社) is a [[:en:Shinto shrine|Shinto shrine]] located in [[:en:Kōriyama|Kōriyama]], [[:en:Fukushima Prefecture|Fukushima Prefecture]]. It is a {{ill|Shikinai shrine|ja|式内社|1=Shikinaisha|2=ja|3=式内社|qq=draft|1=Shikinaisha|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shikinai shrine|13=User:Immanuelle|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyocho|lt=Shikinaisha}} ({{ill|Myōjin taisha|ja|名神大社|fr|Myōjin Taisha|zh|名神大社|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Myōjin taisha|qq=|comment=changed title based on wikidata|1=Myōjin Taisha|WD=Q9610964}}), and its former shrine Ranking under the [[:en:Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|Modern system of Ranked Shinto shrines]] was {{ill|Gōsha|ja|郷社|1=Gō-sha|qid=Q134917316}}{{efn|District Shrines|lt=Ken-sha|1=modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Gōsha|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 近代社格制度|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q712534}}.
[[Unakoro Waki Shrine]] is a regional [[Sōja shrine|Sōja shrine]]. It enshrines many kami of the local region.

It contrasts with provincial Soja shrines which enshrine all the kami of a province<ref>https://archive.ph/wip/XtZfZ</ref>

== Enshrined Deities ==
'''Main deity'''
* '''{{ill|Seoritsu-hime|lt=Seoritsu-hime|lt=Seoritsuhime|ja|瀬織津比売命|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Seoritsuhime|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}''' - One of the {{ill|Haraedo no Kami|lt=Haraedo no Kami|lt=Haraedo-no-Ōkami|ja|祓戸大神|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Haraedo-no-Ōkami|qq=}}

'''Secondary deity'''
* [[:en:Hachiman|Hachiman]]: Hondawake-no-Mikoto ([[:en:Emperor Ōjin|Emperor Ōjin]])

== History ==
=== Founding ===
According to ancient records, during the reign of [[:en:Emperor Kōnin|Emperor Kōnin]], disturbances caused by the [[:en:Emishi|Emishi]] were widespread in the Mutsu Province. In the first year of {{ill|Ten'ō|ja|天応 (日本)|1=Ten'ō|2=ja|3=天応 (日本)|qq=|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q1373125}}{{citation needed|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ten'ō|qq=}} ([[:en:781|781]]), the court dispatched {{ill|Fujiwara no Oguro|ja|藤原小黒麿|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Fujiwara no Oguro|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}} as inspector of Mutsu and Dewa, but he failed to suppress the unrest. The following year, in the first year of [[:en:Enryaku|Enryaku]] ([[:en:782|782]]), the newly enthroned [[:en:Emperor Kanmu|Emperor Kanmu]] sent [[:en:Ōtomo no Yakamochi|Ōtomo no Yakamochi]] to the region as an inspector and commander. After seeking divine guidance on {{ill|Takahatayama|ja|高旗山|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Takahatayama|qq=|1=Takahata Yama|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q31470237}}, Yakamochi established a shrine to enshrine Seoritsuhime, whose divine power reportedly aided in subduing the unrest.

Later, in the 3rd year of Enryaku ([[:en:784|784]]), the shrine was moved from Takahatayama to its current location in Yamazaki. At that time, {{ill|Hachiman|ja|八幡大神|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Hachiman|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 八幡神}} (Hondawake-no-Mikoto) was enshrined as a secondary deity. A stone shrine (okumiya) remains on the summit of Takahatayama, and an information board at the mountain trailhead near {{ill|Genda Onsen|ja|源田温泉|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Genda Onsen|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}} recounts this history.

According to shrine records, the shrine’s origins trace back to the 11th year of Emperor Kinmei ([[:en:550|550]]?). During that time, Seoritsuhime was believed to have manifested on Takahatayama's summit. Seoritsuhime, identified as a deity born from the purification of {{ill|Izanagi|ja|伊耶那岐命|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Izanagi|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}} at Awagihara, was venerated for her ability to cleanse impurities and pacify disturbances. 

=== Historical Overview ===
As recorded in the [[:en:Shoku Nihon Kōki|Shoku Nihongi]], the shrine was granted the Rank of Junior Fifth Rank, Lower Grade, in the 14th year of [[:en:Jōwa|Jōwa (Heian period)]] ([[:en:847|847]]). According to the [[:en:Ruijū Kokushi|Ruijū Kokushi]], the shrine's Rank was elevated to Senior Fifth Rank, Lower Grade, in the 11th year of [[:en:Zhenguan|Jōgan]] ([[:en:869|869]]).

In [[:en:927|927]], the shrine was listed as a {{ill|Myōjin taisha|ja|名神大社|fr|Myōjin Taisha|zh|名神大社|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Myōjin taisha|qq=|comment=changed title based on wikidata|1=Myōjin Taisha|WD=Q9610964}} in the [[:en:Engishiki|Engishiki]] and became the main shrine of the three {{ill|Shikinaisha|ja|式内社|1=Shikinaisha|2=ja|3=式内社|qq=draft|1=Shikinaisha|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shikinaisha|13=User:Immanuelle|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyocho|lt=Shikinaisha}} in the Asaka District, receiving deep veneration from both rulers and local residents. It was also designated the total shrine (sōsha) for the {{ill|33 communities of Asaka|ja|安積三十三郷|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/33 communities of Asaka|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}.

During the [[:en:Kamakura period|Kamakura period]], [[:en:Kudō Suketsune|Kudō Suketsune]], appointed as local governor, introduced deities from the [[:en:Mishima Taisha|Mishima Taisha]] and other shrines in Izu Province as subordinate shrines. The shrine was further restored by local rulers such as [[:en:Ashina clan (Japan)|Ashina clan]] and [[:en:Gamō Ujisato|Gamo Ujisato]], and it received land grants and other support.

=== typeings ===
* [[:en:Jōwa|Jōwa (Heian period)]] 14 ([[:en:847|847]]): Junior Fifth Rank, Lower Grade ([[:en:Shoku Nihon Kōki|Shoku Nihongi]])
* [[:en:Zhenguan|Jōgan]] 11 ([[:en:869|869]]): Senior Fifth Rank, Lower Grade ([[:en:Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku|Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku]])

== Shrine Grounds ==
=== Main Shrine ===
The shrine comprises a haiden, heiden, and a covered three-bay honden in [[:en:Nagare-zukuri|Miya-zukuri]] style. The honden features three doors, symbolizing the deities enshrined. Presently, the shrine honors two deities, with one seat left vacant.

<gallery widths="200px" heights="150px">
File:Honden of Unakorowake-jinja 1.JPG|Honden (Three-bay miya-zukuri style)
File:Unakorowake-jinja 1.JPG|Haiden
</gallery>

=== Torii and Approach ===
A stream flows beneath the torii. The approach features memorial stones, subsidiary shrines, and a shrine office.

<gallery>
File:Suitengu shrine of Unakorowake-jinja.JPG|Water deity shrine
File:Main approach to Unakorowake-jinja 2.JPG|Main approach
File:Torii gate of Unakorowake-jinja.JPG|Torii
</gallery>

=== Subsidiary Shrines ===
The shrine precinct includes various stone shrines dedicated to deities such as {{ill|Mishima Taisha|ja|三嶋神社|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Mishima Taisha|qq=|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 三島神社}}, [[:en:Izusan Shrine|Izu-san Shrine]], [[:en:Hakone Shrine|Hakone Shrine]], and others.

== Festivals ==
The Spring Festival is held annually from [[:en:April 2|April 2]], and the Autumn Festival occurs from [[:en:September 18|September 18]]. Historical records indicate that events such as horseback archery were once part of the celebrations.

== Access ==
'''Address'''
* [[:en:Fukushima Prefecture|Fukushima Prefecture]], [[:en:Kōriyama|Kōriyama]], Mihota-machi Hachiman, Kamino-dai 76

=== Public Transportation ===
* From [[:en:Kōriyama Station|Koriyama Station]], take the No. 2 bus bound for Nagahama (approx. 35 minutes). Alight at Mihota Community Center and walk immediately.

=== By Car ===
