'''Unakorowake Shrine''' (宇奈己呂和気神社) is a {{ill|Shinto shrine|en|Shinto shrine|WD=Q845945|lt=Shinto shrine|1=Shinto shrine}} located in {{ill|Kōriyama|en|Kōriyama|WD=Q264206|lt=Kōriyama|1=Kōriyama}}, {{ill|Fukushima Prefecture|en|Fukushima Prefecture|WD=Q71707|lt=Fukushima Prefecture|1=Fukushima Prefecture}}. It is a {{ill|Shikinai shrine|ja|式内社|1=Shikinaisha|2=ja|3=式内社|1=Shikinaisha|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shikinai shrine|13=User:Immanuelle|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyocho|lt=Shikinaisha}} ({{ill|Myōjin taisha|ja|名神大社|fr|Myōjin Taisha|zh|名神大社|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Myōjin taisha|comment=changed title based on wikidata|1=Myōjin Taisha|WD=Q9610964}}), and its former shrine Ranking under the {{ill|Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|en|Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|WD=Q712534|lt=Modern system of Ranked Shinto shrines|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines}} was {{ill|Gōsha|ja|郷社|1=Gō-sha|WD=Q134917316}}{{efn|District Shrines|WD=Q90139226|lt=Ken-sha|1=modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Gōsha|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 近代社格制度|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q712534}}.
[[Unakoro Waki Shrine]] is a regional [[Sōja shrine|Sōja shrine]]. It enshrines many kami of the local region.

It contrasts with provincial Soja shrines which enshrine all the kami of a province<ref>https://archive.ph/wip/XtZfZ</ref>

== Enshrined Deities ==
'''Main deity'''
* '''{{ill|Seoritsu-hime|lt=Seoritsu-hime|lt=Seoritsuhime|ja|瀬織津比売命|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Seoritsuhime|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}''' - One of the {{ill|Haraedo no Kami|lt=Haraedo no Kami|lt=Haraedo-no-Ōkami|ja|祓戸大神|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Haraedo-no-Ōkami|qq=}}

'''Secondary deity'''
* {{ill|Hachiman|en|Hachiman|WD=Q261637|lt=Hachiman|1=Hachiman}}: Hondawake-no-Mikoto ({{ill|Emperor Ōjin|en|Emperor Ōjin|WD=Q317997|lt=Emperor Ōjin|1=Emperor Ōjin}})

== History ==
=== Founding ===
According to ancient records, during the reign of {{ill|Emperor Kōnin|en|Emperor Kōnin|WD=Q958662|lt=Emperor Kōnin|1=Emperor Kōnin}}, disturbances caused by the {{ill|Emishi|en|Emishi|WD=Q932574|lt=Emishi|1=Emishi}} were widespread in the Mutsu Province. In the first year of {{ill|Ten'ō|ja|天応 (日本)|1=Ten'ō|2=ja|3=天応 (日本)|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q1373125}}{{citation needed|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ten'ō|qq=}} ({{ill|781|en|781|WD=Q31175|lt=781|1=781}}), the court dispatched {{ill|Fujiwara no Oguro|ja|藤原小黒麿|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Fujiwara no Oguro|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}} as inspector of Mutsu and Dewa, but he failed to suppress the unrest. The following year, in the first year of {{ill|Enryaku|en|Enryaku|WD=Q1373176|lt=Enryaku|1=Enryaku}} ({{ill|782|en|782|WD=Q30550|lt=782|1=782}}), the newly enthroned {{ill|Emperor Kanmu|en|Emperor Kanmu|WD=Q314846|lt=Emperor Kanmu|1=Emperor Kanmu}} sent {{ill|Ōtomo no Yakamochi|en|Ōtomo no Yakamochi|WD=Q386308|lt=Ōtomo no Yakamochi|1=Ōtomo no Yakamochi}} to the region as an inspector and commander. After seeking divine guidance on {{ill|Takahatayama|ja|高旗山|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Takahatayama|1=Takahata Yama|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q31470237}}, Yakamochi established a shrine to enshrine Seoritsuhime, whose divine power reportedly aided in subduing the unrest.

Later, in the 3rd year of Enryaku ({{ill|784|en|784|WD=Q30083|lt=784|1=784}}), the shrine was moved from Takahatayama to its current location in Yamazaki. At that time, {{ill|Hachiman|ja|八幡大神|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Hachiman|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 八幡神}} (Hondawake-no-Mikoto) was enshrined as a secondary deity. A stone shrine (okumiya) remains on the summit of Takahatayama, and an information board at the mountain trailhead near {{ill|Genda Onsen|ja|源田温泉|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Genda Onsen|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}} recounts this history.

According to shrine records, the shrine’s origins trace back to the 11th year of Emperor Kinmei ({{ill|550|en|550|WD=Q32150|lt=550|1=550}}?). During that time, Seoritsuhime was believed to have manifested on Takahatayama's summit. Seoritsuhime, identified as a deity born from the purification of {{ill|Izanagi|ja|伊耶那岐命|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Izanagi|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}} at Awagihara, was venerated for her ability to cleanse impurities and pacify disturbances. 

=== Historical Overview ===
As recorded in the {{ill|Shoku Nihon Kōki|en|Shoku Nihon Kōki|WD=Q1041301|lt=Shoku Nihongi|1=Shoku Nihon Kōki}}, the shrine was granted the Rank of Junior Fifth Rank, Lower Grade, in the 14th year of {{ill|Jōwa|en|Jōwa|WD=Q3577279|lt=Jōwa (Heian period)|1=Jōwa}} ({{ill|847|en|847|WD=Q30354|lt=847|1=847}}). According to the {{ill|Ruijū Kokushi|en|Ruijū Kokushi|WD=Q1038639|lt=Ruijū Kokushi|1=Ruijū Kokushi}}, the shrine's Rank was elevated to Senior Fifth Rank, Lower Grade, in the 11th year of {{ill|Zhenguan|en|Zhenguan|WD=Q3577278|lt=Jōgan|1=Zhenguan}} ({{ill|869|en|869|WD=Q23917|lt=869|1=869}}).

In {{ill|927|en|927|WD=Q30470|lt=927|1=927}}, the shrine was listed as a {{ill|Myōjin taisha|ja|名神大社|fr|Myōjin Taisha|zh|名神大社|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Myōjin taisha|comment=changed title based on wikidata|1=Myōjin Taisha|WD=Q9610964}} in the {{ill|Engishiki|en|Engishiki|WD=Q1342448|lt=Engishiki|1=Engishiki}} and became the main shrine of the three {{ill|Shikinaisha|ja|式内社|1=Shikinaisha|2=ja|3=式内社|1=Shikinaisha|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shikinaisha|13=User:Immanuelle|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyocho|lt=Shikinaisha}} in the Asaka District, receiving deep veneration from both rulers and local residents. It was also designated the total shrine (sōsha) for the {{ill|33 communities of Asaka|ja|安積三十三郷|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/33 communities of Asaka|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}.

During the {{ill|Kamakura period|en|Kamakura period|WD=Q236205|lt=Kamakura period|1=Kamakura period}}, {{ill|Kudō Suketsune|en|Kudō Suketsune|WD=Q11479615|lt=Kudō Suketsune|1=Kudō Suketsune}}, appointed as local governor, introduced deities from the {{ill|Mishima Taisha|en|Mishima Taisha|WD=Q650772|lt=Mishima Taisha|1=Mishima Taisha}} and other shrines in Izu Province as subordinate shrines. The shrine was further restored by local rulers such as {{ill|Ashina clan (Japan)|en|Ashina clan (Japan)|WD=Q2975229|lt=Ashina clan|1=Ashina clan (Japan)}} and {{ill|Gamō Ujisato|en|Gamō Ujisato|WD=Q906580|lt=Gamo Ujisato|1=Gamō Ujisato}}, and it received land grants and other support.

=== typeings ===
* {{ill|Jōwa|en|Jōwa|WD=Q3577279|lt=Jōwa (Heian period)|1=Jōwa}} 14 ({{ill|847|en|847|WD=Q30354|lt=847|1=847}}): Junior Fifth Rank, Lower Grade ({{ill|Shoku Nihon Kōki|en|Shoku Nihon Kōki|WD=Q1041301|lt=Shoku Nihongi|1=Shoku Nihon Kōki}})
* {{ill|Zhenguan|en|Zhenguan|WD=Q3577278|lt=Jōgan|1=Zhenguan}} 11 ({{ill|869|en|869|WD=Q23917|lt=869|1=869}}): Senior Fifth Rank, Lower Grade ({{ill|Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku|en|Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku|WD=Q1052981|lt=Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku|1=Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku}})

== Shrine Grounds ==
=== Main Shrine ===
The shrine comprises a haiden, heiden, and a covered three-bay honden in {{ill|Nagare-zukuri|en|Nagare-zukuri|WD=Q210684|lt=Miya-zukuri|1=Nagare-zukuri}} style. The honden features three doors, symbolizing the deities enshrined. Presently, the shrine honors two deities, with one seat left vacant.

<gallery widths="200px" heights="150px">
File:Honden of Unakorowake-jinja 1.JPG|Honden (Three-bay miya-zukuri style)
File:Unakorowake-jinja 1.JPG|Haiden
</gallery>

=== Torii and Approach ===
A stream flows beneath the torii. The approach features memorial stones, subsidiary shrines, and a shrine office.

<gallery>
File:Suitengu shrine of Unakorowake-jinja.JPG|Water deity shrine
File:Main approach to Unakorowake-jinja 2.JPG|Main approach
File:Torii gate of Unakorowake-jinja.JPG|Torii
</gallery>

=== Subsidiary Shrines ===
The shrine precinct includes various stone shrines dedicated to deities such as {{ill|Mishima Taisha|ja|三嶋神社|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Mishima Taisha|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 三島神社}}, {{ill|Izusan Shrine|en|Izusan Shrine|WD=Q611460|lt=Izu-san Shrine|1=Izusan Shrine}}, {{ill|Hakone Shrine|en|Hakone Shrine|WD=Q705310|lt=Hakone Shrine|1=Hakone Shrine}}, and others.

== Festivals ==
The Spring Festival is held annually from {{ill|April 2|en|April 2|WD=Q2511|lt=April 2|1=April 2}}, and the Autumn Festival occurs from {{ill|September 18|en|September 18|WD=Q2893|lt=September 18|1=September 18}}. Historical records indicate that events such as horseback archery were once part of the celebrations.

== Access ==
'''Address'''
* {{ill|Fukushima Prefecture|en|Fukushima Prefecture|WD=Q71707|lt=Fukushima Prefecture|1=Fukushima Prefecture}}, {{ill|Kōriyama|en|Kōriyama|WD=Q264206|lt=Kōriyama|1=Kōriyama}}, Mihota-machi Hachiman, Kamino-dai 76

=== Public Transportation ===
* From {{ill|Kōriyama Station|en|Kōriyama Station|WD=Q1208435|lt=Koriyama Station|1=Kōriyama Station}}, take the No. 2 bus bound for Nagahama (approx. 35 minutes). Alight at Mihota Community Center and walk immediately.

=== By Car ===
