'''Unakorowake Shrine''' (宇奈己呂和気神社) is a {{ill|Shinto shrine|en|Shinto shrine|WD=Q845945|lt=Shinto shrine|1=Shinto shrine}} located in {{ill|Kōriyama|en|Kōriyama|WD=Q264206|lt=Kōriyama|1=Kōriyama}}, {{ill|Fukushima Prefecture|en|Fukushima Prefecture|WD=Q71707|lt=Fukushima Prefecture|1=Fukushima Prefecture}}. It is designated as a {{ill|Shikinaisha|ja|式内社|1=Shikinaisha|2=ja|3=式内社|1=Shikinaisha|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shikinaisha|13=User:Immanuelle|1=Engishiki Jinmyōchō|2=ja|3=延喜式神名帳|4=zh|5=延喜式神名帳|6=simple|7=Engishiki Jinmyocho|lt=Shikinaisha}} ({{ill|Myōjin Taisha|ja|名神大社|fr|Myōjin Taisha|zh|名神大社|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Myōjin Taisha|comment=enwiki is a redirect}}), with its former status being that of a {{ill|Gōsha|ja|郷社|1=Gō-sha|WD=Q134917316}}{{efn|District Shrines|WD=Q90139226|lt=Ken-sha|1=modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Gōsha|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 近代社格制度|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q712534}}.

== Enshrined Deities ==
'''Chief deity'''
* '''{{ill|Seoritsu-hime|lt=Seoritsu-hime|lt=Seoritsu Hime|ja|瀬織津姫|1=Seoritsu-hime|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Seoritsu Hime|13=User:Immanuelle/Seoritsu-hime|qq=}}''' – One of the {{ill|Haraedo no Kami|lt=Haraedo no Kami|lt=Haraedo no Ōkami|ja|祓戸大神|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Haraedo no Ōkami|qq=}} deities

'''Subsidiary Deities'''
* Hachiman God: Homutawake-no-Mikoto ({{ill|Emperor Ōjin|en|Emperor Ōjin|WD=Q317997|lt=Emperor Ōjin|1=Emperor Ōjin}})

== History ==
=== Establishment ===
According to old records of the shrine, during the reign of {{ill|Emperor Kōnin|en|Emperor Kōnin|WD=Q958662|lt=Emperor Kōnin|1=Emperor Kōnin}}, the land of Mutsu Province was rife with disturbances caused by the Emishi. In the first year of Ten'ō (781), the imperial court dispatched {{ill|Fujiwara no Kuro Maro|ja|藤原小黒麻呂|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Fujiwara no Kuro Maro|1=Fujiwara no Oguromaro|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q11623210}} as the Inspector of Mutsu and Dewa Provinces (陸奥出羽按察使), but this effort yielded no results. The following year, in June of the first year of Enryaku (782), the newly enthroned Emperor Kanmu appointed {{ill|Ōtomo no Yakamochi|en|Ōtomo no Yakamochi|WD=Q386308|lt=Ōtomo no Yakamochi|1=Ōtomo no Yakamochi}} as Inspector of Mutsu and Dewa Provinces and Commander of the Defense Forces (鎮守府将軍) and sent him to the region. However, the Emishi's strength was formidable, and Yakamochi ascended {{ill|Takahatayama|ja|高旗山|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Takahatayama|1=Takahata Yama|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q31470237}} to perform purification rituals and offer prayers to the deities. In response, divine spirits manifested, and miraculous signs appeared on the mountains of Asaka and {{ill|Yatsuhatayama|ja|八ツ旗山|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Yatsuhatayama|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}. Emboldened by these divine signs, Yakamochi led a campaign to pacify the Emishi. Through his efforts, the rebellions in Mutsu and Dewa Provinces were subdued, and peace was restored to the land. In gratitude for the divine assistance, Yakamochi constructed a magnificent shrine on the summit of Takahatayama to enshrine the guardian deity. Over time, the shrine fell into disrepair, prompting its relocation to its current site in Yamazaki in the third year of Enryaku (784), where a new shrine building was constructed. The deity {{ill|Hachiman Ōkami|ja|八幡大神|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Hachiman Ōkami|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 八幡神|1=Hachiman|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q261637}} (Homuda Wake no Mikoto) was also enshrined as a secondary deity. At the entrance to the mountain trail (pilgrimage path) leading to Takahatayama, near {{ill|Genda Onsen|ja|郡山市内の温泉#源田温泉（forest バン源田）|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Genda Onsen|1=Q11643189|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q11643189}}, there is a signboard detailing this history, and the summit still houses a stone shrine (okumiya) dedicated to {{ill|Unakiorowake Shrine|ja|宇奈己呂和気神社|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Unakiorowake Shrine|1=Unakorowake Shrine|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q17217367}}.

According to the shrine’s traditions, it was established in the 11th year of Emperor Kinmei's reign (circa 550 CE) when the deity {{ill|Seoritsu-hime|lt=Seoritsu-hime|lt=Seoritsu Hime|ja|瀬織津比売|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Seoritsu Hime|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 瀬織津姫}} manifested on the summit of Takahatayama in Asaka District. In the shrine's records, Seoritsu Hime is identified as the same deity as {{ill|Yaso Magatsuhi no Mikoto|ja|八十禍津日命|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Yaso Magatsuhi no Mikoto|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}, born from the impurities cleansed by {{ill|Izanagi|en|Izanagi|WD=Q813858|lt=Izanagi|1=Izanagi}} during his purification ritual at {{ill|Awagihara|ja|阿波岐原|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Awagihara|ja_comment=jawiki link invalid}}. Furthermore, Seoritsu Hime is associated with the "Ara-mitama" of Amaterasu Ōmikami, worshipped at the {{ill|Aramatsuri-no-Miya|ja|荒祭宮|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Aramatsuri Shrine|qq=}} of {{ill|Ise Shrine|en|Ise Shrine|WD=Q687168|lt=Ise Grand Shrine|1=Ise Grand Shrine}} and {{ill|Hirota Shrine|en|Hirota Shrine|WD=Q1466105|lt=Hirota Shrine|1=Hirota Shrine (Osaka City)}}. She is revered as a deity who purifies sins, impurities, and calamities in the world. This tradition reflects the prayers offered to Seoritsu Hime for the successful pacification of the Mutsu region, which had a fluctuating relationship with the Yamato court. The shrine is said to have been relocated from Takahatayama to its current location in the third year of Enryaku (784).

=== Historical Overview ===
According to the {{ill|Shoku Nihon Kōki|en|Shoku Nihon Kōki|WD=Q1041301|lt=Shoku Nihon Kōki|1=Shoku Nihon Kōki}}, in the 14th year of Shōwa (847), the deity of Unakiorowake Shrine was granted the Rank of Junior Fifth Rank, Lower Grade. The {{ill|Ruijū Kokushi|en|Ruijū Kokushi|WD=Q1038639|lt=Ruijū Kokushi|1=Ruijū Kokushi}} records that in the 11th year of Jōgan (869), this Rank was elevated to Senior Fifth Rank, Lower Grade. Compiled in the 5th year of Enchō (927), the {{ill|Engishiki|en|Engishiki|WD=Q1342448|lt=Engishiki|1=Engishiki}} designated Unakiorowake Shrine as a {{ill|Myōjin Taisha|ja|名神大社|fr|Myōjin Taisha|zh|名神大社|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Myōjin Taisha|comment=enwiki is a redirect}}, one of the major shrines of Asaka District’s three enshrined shrines. As the General Shrine (Sōsha) of the 33 Asaka villages, it commanded enduring respect and devotion from local leaders and residents.

During the Kamakura period, {{ill|Kudō Suketsune|en|Kudō Suketsune|WD=Q11479615|lt=Kudō Suketsune|1=Kudō Suketsune}} was assigned as the local governor of Asaka District. He dispatched his son, {{ill|Asaka Suketaka|ja|安積祐長|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Asaka Suketaka|1=Asaka no Suketaka|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q48761369}}, to manage the region. Recognizing the importance of “guardian worship” to secure public allegiance, Kudō Suketsune introduced deities from {{ill|Mishima Taisha|en|Mishima Taisha|WD=Q650772|lt=Mishima Taisha|1=Mishima Taisha}}, {{ill|Izusan Shrine|en|Izusan Shrine|WD=Q611460|lt=Izusan Shrine|1=Izusan Shrine}}, and {{ill|Hakone Shrine|en|Hakone Shrine|WD=Q705310|lt=Hakone Shrine|1=Hakone Shrine}} into Asaka. Initially, these deities were enshrined as subsidiary gods of Unakiorowake Shrine rather than as principal deities, aiming to stabilize the populace and establish faith. Similar policies were adopted in the 6th year of Ōei (1399) when {{ill|Ashikaga Mitsukane|en|Ashikaga Mitsukane|WD=Q4307089|lt=Ashikaga Mitsukane|1=Ashikaga Mitsukane}} sent {{ill|Ashikaga Mitsunao|ja|足利満直|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ashikaga Mitsunao|1=Ashikaga Mitsunao|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q11636791}} and {{ill|Ashikaga Mitsusada|ja|足利満貞|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ashikaga Mitsusada|1=Ashikaga Mitsusada|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q11636794}} to Mutsu and Dewa to solidify control. Upon arriving in Asaka District, Mitsunao sought to restore Unakiorowake Shrine. In the 9th year of Ōei (1402), he renovated the shrine buildings and grounds and appointed his retainer, Ōhara Yasunobu, as the shrine priest. The Ōhara family continues to serve as shrine priests to this day.

After its relocation from Takahatayama, Hachiman Ōkami was enshrined as a secondary deity, leading to the shrine being known as “Hachiman Shrine” and later, during the Edo period, as “Aidan Hachiman Shrine.” Worship of the martial deity Hachiman Ōkami drew the reverence of local samurai. Historical records, including land donation documents granting 200 koku of land from lords such as Itō Taka-yuki, {{ill|Ashina Moritaka|ja|蘆名盛高|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ashina Moritaka|1=Q11625151|comment=changed title based on wikidata|WD=Q11625151}}, {{ill|Ashina Moriuji|en|Ashina Moriuji|WD=Q837265|lt=Ashina Moriuji|1=Ashina Moriuji}}, and {{ill|Gamō Ujisato|en|Gamō Ujisato|WD=Q906580|lt=Gamo Ujisato|1=Gamō Ujisato}}, as well as prayers from {{ill|Uesugi Kagekatsu|en|Uesugi Kagekatsu|WD=Q1376605|lt=Uesugi Kagekatsu|1=Uesugi Kagekatsu}} before the Battle of Sekigahara in Keichō 5 (1600), and the “Aidan Hachiman Documents,” have been preserved. In the 15th year of Meiji (1882), Unakiorowake Shrine was designated a village shrine (Gōsha).

=== Ranks ===
* Shōwa 14 (847): Elevated from no Rank to Junior Fifth Rank, Lower Grade ({{ill|Shoku Nihon Kōki|en|Shoku Nihon Kōki|WD=Q1041301|lt=Shoku Nihon Kōki|1=Shoku Nihon Kōki}})
* Jōgan 11 (869): Elevated from Junior Fifth Rank, Upper Grade, to Senior Fifth Rank, Lower Grade ({{ill|Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku|en|Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku|WD=Q1052981|lt=Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku|1=Nihon Sandai Jitsuroku}})

== Grounds ==
=== Shrine buildings ===
The shrine complex includes the '''haiden''' (worship hall), '''heiden''' (offering hall), and the '''honden''' (main hall), which is a three-bay nagare-zukuri style structure with an enclosing roof. The '''honden''' features three doors, and according to shrine tradition, in addition to Seoritsu-hime and Hondawake-no-Mikoto, Kasuga Ōkami ({{ill|Ame-no-Koyane-no-Mikoto|ja|天児屋根命|1=Ame-no-Koyane|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ame-no-Koyane-no-Mikoto|13=User:Immanuelle/Ame-no-Koyane|ja_comment=jawiki redirects to 天児屋命}}) was once enshrined here. Currently, the shrine enshrines only Seoritsu-hime and Hondawake-no-Mikoto, leaving one seat vacant.  
